---
/**
 * hospitality / HeaderBK — THE STICKY RAIL. The design's front gate,
 * reproduced: a 90%-opaque night bar over a 14px backdrop blur, a hairline
 * --rule under it, and one flex row at 19px top and bottom holding three
 * things — the Prata wordmark with its brass region tag on the baseline
 * beside it, five 13px wayfinding links, and the brass pill.
 *
 * ROLE: wayfinding plus the one action this page exists for. The pill is a
 * SIBLING of the link row, never a child of it: the link row becomes a
 * display:none drawer at <=900px and the enquiry affordance must not leave
 * with it.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string on this rail, and nothing else:
 *   footer.brand                     — the wordmark (also the footer's; one
 *                                      field, two places, on purpose)
 *   footer.locality                  — the brass region tag beside it
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — one nav link's label
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href           — that link's target (data-href-field)
 *   navCtaText                       — the brass pill's label
 *   navCtaHref                       — the pill's target (data-href-field)
 * All six are declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts under the
 * `header` group, so each one is a hook a sidebar input actually owns.
 *
 * DERIVED, never authored here:
 *   - the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when content.footer.brand
 *     is blank, because that is the same name from the same submission.
 *   - every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBK really
 *     renders (see the anchor guard below).
 *   - `i` is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index of a nav link, so dropping an
 *     unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "The villa", "Included", "Gallery", "Location",
 *     "FAQ", "Contact us" and "Villa Marindu · Palawan" are the designer
 *     filling in their own template. Not one of those words is in this file. A
 *     site whose owner supplied no nav links gets no nav links.
 *   - THE WHOLE RAIL, when brand, locality, links and pill are all empty.
 *     A sticky bar with nothing in it is 84px of dead chrome on every screen.
 *   - the burger, unless there is a drawer for it to open.
 *   - the wordmark, with no name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real
 *     customer's own wordmark; the region tag then carries the lock alone.
 *   - a nav link with no label, or whose href reaches no section on this page.
 *     A 0-width tap target and a link that scrolls nowhere are both broken.
 *   Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a blank value removes the ELEMENT.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE READ AS OVERSIGHTS
 *
 * 1. WHY THE BACKGROUND IS A PSEUDO-ELEMENT. The design's rail is
 *    rgba(16,20,19,.9) — that is --night at 90%. The wrapper publishes
 *    --scrim-rgb and --bone-rgb but no --night-rgb, and rgba(11,14,13,.9) is
 *    a different colour from the one the design specified. So the tint is
 *    --night painted flat on .rail::before at opacity .9, and the blur sits on
 *    .rail itself: same pixels, no literal anywhere, and it degrades to a
 *    plain 90% night bar where backdrop-filter is unsupported.
 *
 * 2. WHY THE PILL HOOKS navCtaText AND NOT hero.cta1.text.
 *    components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle to a section by
 *    counting hooks of that prefix, so a header carrying hero.* hooks vanishes
 *    when the owner toggles the Hero block off. navCtaText/navCtaHref belong to
 *    the Header group, so this rail answers only to itself. footer.brand is
 *    safe twice over: the bridge only ever hides `closest('section,footer')`
 *    and a <header> is neither, and the real footer outweighs this file's two
 *    footer.* hooks many times over.
 *    UNLIKE HeaderBJ, the pill does NOT fall back to hero.cta1.text for its
 *    DISPLAY. Showing a label the Header-CTA box does not contain means the
 *    sidebar and the page disagree about what the button says; an empty box
 *    should render an empty rail, not the hero's words.
 *
 * 3. WHY THE WORDMARK LOCK HAS NO aria-label. Its accessible name is its own
 *    text — the owner's name and the owner's region. HeaderBJ appends the
 *    literal "home"; that is a word this file did not receive and will not
 *    invent. The two invisible strings that ARE written here, "Primary" on the
 *    nav landmark and "Menu" on the icon-only burger, are a11y affordances in
 *    the same class as the wrapper's skip link, never page copy.
 *
 * 4. WHY THE REGION TAG IS --accent. The design puts it in brass, as it does
 *    every eyebrow on the page (6.62:1 on --night). --accent is the one token
 *    PageBK hands to the theme engine, so an admin who picks a very dark
 *    scheme dims this tag along with every other eyebrow in the template. That
 *    is a template-wide axis the wrapper accepted on purpose; re-deriving it
 *    HERE alone would leave the header brass-less on a page still full of
 *    brass. The nav-link hover is --accent-light, the design's own #D2B173 —
 *    and it rides the SAME axis, not around it: buildOverrideCss aliases
 *    --accent-light to palette.accent, which is lighter than palette.primary in
 *    most schemes but not all (mono and white map it darker, yellow maps it to
 *    a red at 3.79:1 on night). It is a hover-only state over a rest colour of
 *    --grey at 8.30:1, so the honest place to fix it is the wrapper's accent
 *    handling, never a re-derivation inside this one file.
 *
 * 5. WHY flex:none BECAME flex:0 1 auto ON THE LOCK. The design says flex:none,
 *    which at 360px lets a long single-word property name shove the pill off
 *    screen. The lock shrinks and the wordmark ellipsises instead; at desktop
 *    widths the rendering is identical to the design.
 *
 * 6. target="_blank" ONLY FOR http(s). The design's CTA opens a messenger in a
 *    new tab. An owner may point this at #location or a tel: instead, and
 *    hijacking those would be wrong, so the attribute pair is computed, never
 *    literal — and it is one conditional, so no duplicated attribute.
 *
 * Never visibility-gated: there is no header toggle any PageXX honours.
 * Buttons here are .btn + .btn--fill from the wrapper. Not .nav-cta, not
 * .btn-primary, not inside .nav-right — buildOverrideCss pins the label colour
 * on all three with !important.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const c = (content as any) || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes nav links to '#about', '#services',
// '#why', '#work', '#visit' and navCtaHref to '#visit', and lib/astro-builder.ts
// falls back to '#about', '#services', '#gallery', '#contact' — ids most designs
// never render, so those links scrolled nowhere. Resolve every in-page href
// against what PageBK ACTUALLY renders: keep it if the id exists, remap it when
// this design carries the same section under another name, otherwise fall back
// (nav links pass '' and are dropped). Off-page hrefs pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['about', 'services', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames four derived targets: '#services' is what is INCLUDED in
// the let (rooms/amenities land there too), '#about' is the villa itself (host
// and why are the same block here), '#work' is the photo mosaic, and '#visit' /
// '#contact' both mean the location block — address, map and house rules. A
// booking link has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  amenities: 'services',
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'location',
  host: 'about',
  rooms: 'services',
  visit: 'location',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. 'Your Business' was a shipped placeholder that rendered as the
// customer's own name — with nothing supplied the mark is omitted instead.
// `||`, never `??`: genericContentSchema gives footer.brand
// fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks
// treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand box still shows the business name in
// the sidebar. With `??` an empty string is a value, and the page would have
// gone wordmark-less while the sidebar displayed a name — exactly the
// page/sidebar disagreement decision 2 below refuses for the pill.
const brand = String(c.footer?.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// The region tag. One field, three consumers (here, the footer wordmark and the
// map caption) and nothing derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent
// rather than guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(c.footer?.locality ?? '').trim();

// Nav links. `i` is the ORIGINAL index so per-link edits stay aimed at the row
// the owner clicked even after unreachable rows are dropped.
const navLinks: Array<{ href: string; label: string; i: number }> =
  (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : [])
    .map((l: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(), href: onPage(l?.href, ''), i }))
    .filter((l: { href: string; label: string }) => l.href && l.label);

// The brass pill. Label and target both come from the Header group; an empty
// label removes the whole element rather than leaving a brass rectangle.
const ctaText = String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
// '#location' is the fallback because that is where this design puts the
// address, the map and the house rules — the page has no checkout to send a
// booking link to.
const ctaHref = onPage(c.navCtaHref, '#location');
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// The rail renders only when it carries something. Not a tag, not a headline —
// there is no derived default that can fake all four of these at once.
const hasRail = Boolean(brand || locality || navLinks.length > 0 || ctaText);
---
{hasRail && (
  <header class="rail">
    <div class="wrap rail-in">
      {(brand || locality) && (
        <a class="wm-lock" href="#main">
          {brand && <span class="wm" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
          {locality && <span class="loc" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
        </a>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <nav class="rail-nav" id="rail-nav-BK" aria-label="Primary">
          {navLinks.map((link) => (
            <a
              href={link.href}
              data-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.label`}
              data-href-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.href`}
            >{link.label}</a>
          ))}
        </nav>
      )}
      {/* OUTSIDE .rail-nav on purpose: .rail-nav is a display:none drawer at
          <=900px and the one action this template exists for must survive it. */}
      {ctaText && (
        <a
          href={ctaHref}
          class="btn btn--fill railcta"
          target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
          rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
          data-field="navCtaText"
          data-href-field="navCtaHref"
        >{ctaText}</a>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <button class="rail-burger" type="button" aria-label="Menu" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="rail-nav-BK">
          <span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  </header>
)}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Null-guarded rather than conditionally rendered: an is:inline script must
    // stay at the top level, and with no rail (or no drawer) it simply exits.
    var rail = document.querySelector('header.rail');
    if (!rail) return;
    var btn = rail.querySelector('.rail-burger');
    if (!btn) return;
    function setOpen(open) {
      rail.classList.toggle('open', open);
      btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
    }
    btn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!rail.classList.contains('open')); });
    // '.railcta' is in this selector because the pill lives OUTSIDE the drawer:
    // tapping it while the drawer is open must still close the drawer.
    rail.querySelectorAll('.rail-nav a, .railcta').forEach(function (a) {
      a.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
<style>
  /* The design: sticky, 90% night, blur(14px), one hairline under it. The tint
     is a pseudo-element at opacity .9 because the wrapper publishes no
     --night-rgb and --scrim-rgb is a different colour (see note 1 above). The
     blur stays on .rail so opacity and backdrop-filter never share an element.
     .rail is a stacking context (sticky + z-index), so the -1 cannot escape. */
  .rail {
    /* z-index is the design's own 50. Nothing else in this template stacks
       above 2, and the wrapper's skip link sits at 100, so the rail clears the
       page and the skip link still clears the rail. */
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 50;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
  }
  .rail::before {
    content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1;
    background-color: var(--night); opacity: .9;
  }

  /* The design's row: 19px top and bottom, 24px gap, three parts spread apart.
     NOT position:relative — the <=900px drawer is absolutely positioned and
     resolves against .rail so it spans the full rail width, not the wrap.
     justify-content is flex-end rather than the design's space-between because
     the auto margins below already reproduce space-between whenever two or more
     parts are in flow (an auto margin eats the free space before
     justify-content ever sees it). It only takes effect in the degenerate cases
     the design never drew: a site with no brand and no locality, where at
     <=900px the drawer has left the flow and the pill and burger would
     otherwise sit hard LEFT. */
  .rail-in { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: flex-end; gap: 24px; padding: 19px 0; }

  /* Wordmark + region tag share a baseline, exactly as the design draws them.
     Both the lock and the link row take an auto right margin: with all three
     present that reproduces the design's space-between, and with either one
     missing the pill still sits hard right. */
  .wm-lock {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 11px;
    margin-right: auto; flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0;
  }
  .wm {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 25px;
    line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: .02em; color: var(--bone);
    /* A long single-word property name must not push the pill off a 360px
       screen; flex items are blockified, so ellipsis applies here. */
    min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .loc {
    flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .36em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }

  .rail-nav { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 29px; margin-right: auto; }
  .rail-nav a {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 400; font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--grey);
    transition: color .18s ease;
  }
  /* The design's own hover, straight from its stylesheet: #D2B173, which is
     --accent-light. That token is on the same theme-engine axis as --accent
     rather than immune to it — see note 4 in the frontmatter. */
  .rail-nav a:hover { color: var(--accent-light); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .rail-nav a { transition: none; } }

  /* The design's btnNav is the wrapper's .btn--fill at a smaller size: brass
     fill, --on-accent label, 2px corner, 12px/.16em caps, 13px/24px padding.
     Only the metrics are restated — no fill, no label colour, no box-shadow, so
     the wrapper's hover darkening and its focus ring both stay intact.
     min-height is the ONE value that is not verbatim: btnNav declares none and
     lands about 40px tall, under the 44px target size, and the wrapper's .btn
     would otherwise force 48px. 44px is the closest height to the design that
     is still a legal tap target. */
  .railcta { flex: none; padding: 13px 24px; min-height: 44px; }

  .rail-burger {
    display: none; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; gap: 5px;
    width: 46px; height: 44px; padding: 11px 10px; flex-shrink: 0;
    background-color: transparent; border: 1px solid rgba(var(--bone-rgb),.4);
    border-radius: var(--r-ctl); cursor: pointer;
  }
  .rail-burger span {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: 2px; background-color: var(--bone);
    transition: transform .2s ease, opacity .2s ease;
  }
  header.rail.open .rail-burger span:nth-child(1) { transform: translateY(7px) rotate(45deg); }
  header.rail.open .rail-burger span:nth-child(2) { opacity: 0; }
  header.rail.open .rail-burger span:nth-child(3) { transform: translateY(-7px) rotate(-45deg); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .rail-burger span { transition: none; } }

  /* ONE collapse width: the row leaves and the burger arrives together, so
     there is never a band where neither is on screen. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .rail-burger { display: flex; }
    .rail-nav {
      position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: 100%;
      display: none; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: 0;
      background-color: var(--night-2);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      box-shadow: 0 14px 30px rgba(var(--scrim-rgb),.55);
      padding: 4px 0 10px; max-height: 78vh; overflow-y: auto;
    }
    header.rail.open .rail-nav { display: flex; }
    .rail-nav a {
      display: flex; align-items: center; min-height: 48px; padding: 12px 22px;
      font-size: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .rail-in { gap: 10px; padding: 14px 0; }
    .wm { font-size: 21px; }
    .loc { letter-spacing: .24em; }
    /* display:block, not inline-flex, so text-overflow actually applies; 12px
       padding over a 20px line box still clears a 44px target. */
    .railcta {
      display: block; line-height: 20px; padding: 12px 15px;
      font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .1em;
      max-width: 42vw; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
    }
  }
  /* At 360px the region tag is the first thing to go: the wordmark, the pill
     and the burger are all load-bearing, the tag is a flourish. */
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .loc { display: none; }
    .railcta { max-width: 38vw; padding: 12px 12px; }
  }
</style>
